<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kosovo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kosovo is a landlocked region in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pristina">Pristina</a> and a large Albanian majority. It was a heartland of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and the scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1389, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912–1913. After the war of 1998–1999 it came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> administration, and it declared independence from Serbia in 2008.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kosovo.jpg" alt="Kosovo" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cradel (current version), earlier version by Ningyou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kosovo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Armenia ratified the Treaty on Conventional Armed Forces in Europe in 1992, acceded to the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty in 1993, joined the Collective Security Treaty Organisation and sent troops to NATO's KFOR in <b>Kosovo</b> in 2004; Russia keeps a base at Gyumri as a deterrent against Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2016 about 700 troops were stationed abroad: 100 with the NATO-led ISAF force in Afghanistan, 210 in <b>Kosovo</b> under KFOR and 160 in Bosnia and Herzegovina.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom united some twelve million people in 1921, rising to about sixteen million by 1941, from regions with very different pasts: former Habsburg Slovenia, Croatia, Vojvodina and Bosnia-Herzegovina, and the formerly Ottoman lands of Serbia, Montenegro, <b>Kosovo</b> and Macedonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Within its neutrality Austria has a tradition of UN-led peacekeeping and humanitarian missions, with larger contingents in Bosnia and <b>Kosovo</b> and the all-volunteer Austrian Forces Disaster Relief Unit, able to deploy in ten hours.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Finnish diplomats and soldiers took part in United Nations peacekeeping, and former president Martti Ahtisaari received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2008 for his mediation in Namibia, Aceh and <b>Kosovo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The regions diverged: in 1965 Slovenia's income per head was 177.3% of the national figure and <b>Kosovo</b>'s 38.6%.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
